{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "id": "t13-delegate-batch-isolated-failure-order-preserved",
  "description": "T13 bilateral CLI observation (expected match), item 22 of the versioned inventory (contract L17): a delegate_task batch of three — kid2 fails with an HTTP 418 upstream canary, kid1 and kid3 succeed — settles in the exact reverse of submission order (kid3, then kid2, then kid1), forced by an in-process barrier chain (kid3 opens g2 unconditionally, kid2 waits on g2 then opens g1, kid1 waits on g1), never by sleep/wall-clock. Proves both L17 halves at once: the isolated failure does not abort the batch or raise, and results preserves task-submission order — [ONE-DONE, kid2's error output, THREE-DONE] — regardless of completion order or which task failed, on both the candidate's Promise.all and the oracle's ThreadPoolExecutor-future join. KNOWN LIMIT (declared, not a gap to fix): events.requests is deliberately excluded from capture/comparisons here. recordRequest assigns each request's seq at ARRIVAL, before any gate wait, so with three children admitted concurrently the raw request-log ARRIVAL order is a scheduler artifact, not a semantic guarantee the barrier orders — it is not byte-stable run to run and must not be compared. The order-preservation proof lives entirely in process.stdout's /tool_calls/0/result (task-index order) plus events.summary's post/get counts, both of which are stable by construction, independent of arrival interleaving.",
